# 136 · No idle lanes: a round doesn't wait for another lane's trial, and a refused page isn't fetched again and again

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Running campaigns

## What

A lane that can work must not sit idle. In a research run on the lab, 2026-10-24 and 25, two of six questions got no work for nine hours, and the review panel was never asked, because every question needs an answer card first. Five causes, each a fix:

1. **A round waited for every lane's trials.** Each lane ran its trials of the round, then waited until every other lane's trials had ended and nothing ran. One long trial held every other lane. Now a round ends once every free lane has run its trials. A lane still on a trial joins the next round when its trial ends. Unless the run keeps working (goal [135](135-lanes-and-judge-throughput.goal)), a change waiting for the judge still holds the round, so the next plan has its verdict, and it holds its own lane's next trial in whatever round that is. An experiment the round gave a lane that never started in it stays up next. In a research run, a task the round holds for a lane still on an earlier round's trial does not count toward its question's share of the round (`heldHandouts`), so a free lane can take another task for that question. Code: `src/harness/agents/scheduler/rounds.ts`, used by the scheduler's tick for each mode.
2. **A refused page was fetched again and again.** A worker asked for the same PDF, larger than the run's `fetch_policy.max_bytes`, every two minutes for hours, and each time it was downloaded again and got only `larger than 5000000 bytes`. Now a refusal for a reason that lasts (size, scope, `robots.txt`, a private address) is kept for the run: asked again, the URL is refused at once, with no download, and the answer says it was refused before, how many times it was asked for, and to look for the same text elsewhere. A failure that can pass, such as a timeout, is not kept. Code: `src/harness/research/refusals.ts`. The fetcher's size refusal also had a fault: when the last chunk crossed the limit, the refusal was thrown where nothing caught it, and the fetch returned a body cut short. It now refuses first and then closes the request.
3. **A drain waits on a stuck trial, and nothing says which.** An image update drained the rig and waited 5¼ hours on the stuck trial, with no new trial on any lane. Now the rig's page says it. A card at the top gives since when the rig drains, who asked and why, and what it waits for, with **End the drain** (`drain_rig`, `drain: false`). Under **At work**, each trial it waits for has a row under its lane: how long it has run, its newest tool call in Live and the answer, a page it keeps asking for that the run refused for a reason that lasts (`repeats a refused request`), its work so far, and **Stop this trial…**. That sends the new signed command `stop_trial`, the owner's and an operator's: the trial ends as interrupted, and its experiment goes back to the top of the queue. While a rig drains, its report lists each open trial (`work.open`), and `work.draining` says who asked (`by`). Code: `src/harness/service/drain.ts`, `src/harness/agents/scheduler/stops.ts`, `src/control/ui/rigs/drain.ts`.
4. **The progress rule's clock counted time when no trial could run.** The run's time without an improvement grew through the drain. Time when the run's agents were stopped already did not count (goal [079](_completed/079-thermite-overhead.goal)). Now the research clock stops while the rig drains too, from the `rig_drain` that starts the drain to the one that ends it, or to the first trial started after it (a draining rig starts none, and a harness older than this recorded no end at its restart). As it starts, the harness records the end of a drain that its restart ended (`rig_drain`, `drain: false`, `the harness started again`). Code: `src/harness/service/drain.ts`, and `src/harness/research/blocked.ts`, which the clock reads.
5. **The progress meter reads like a countdown that ran out.** **Without an improvement** showed 5.6 h of 2.2 h, full and in the warning colour, while the rule could not apply (no question was saturated). The owner looked at two other layouts beside today's bar and kept today's bar: its line under the bar already says the rule's state in words, such as `It does not apply yet: Question 1 is not saturated, so the plan still gives it tasks.` No change.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-25, on a lane that had run its trials and waited: "wtf explain lane-1 used its 3 trials and now just decides to never do fucking work again? idle lanes are the destroyer of progress... so yeah we need to fix this crap."

## Constraints

- **No default caps:** none of these fixes ends a trial for its length, turns or time. A trial runs until it ends ([rules for every goal](../README.md#rules-for-every-goal)).
- **The judge's wait stays as the owner set it** in goal 135: a run that doesn't keep working still waits for verdicts.
- **A mockup first** for items 3 and 5, which change screens.

## Done when

- [x] Item 1: a round ends without waiting for other lanes' running trials, in hill-climb, goal and research runs, with the judge's wait kept; tests that fail on the old code (`tests/harness/agents/scheduler/epochs.test.ts`, `scheduler-research.test.ts`, `scheduler/rounds.test.ts`).
- [x] Item 2: a lasting refusal is answered at once when asked again, with no download; the fetcher's size refusal is caught; a test (`tests/harness/research/research.test.ts`).
- [x] Items 1 and 2 rolled to the lab, and a research run there shows no lane idle behind another's trial.
- [x] Item 3, after the owner approved its mockup, with tests that fail on the old code (`tests/harness/service/drain.test.ts`, `tests/harness/agents/scheduler/stops.test.ts`, `tests/control/ui/rigs/drain.test.ts`).
- [x] Item 4, with tests that fail on the old code (`tests/harness/service/drain.test.ts`, `tests/harness/research/research.test.ts`).
- [x] Item 5, after the owner looked at its mockups: today's bar stays.
- [ ] Items 3 and 4 rolled to the lab: a drain there lists the trials it waits for, and a research run's clock leaves the drain out.
- [x] The pages match the code for each item.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-25, owner: idle lanes are not acceptable. A round must not hold a free lane behind another lane's trial.
- 2026-10-25, lead, within goal 079's rule that the research clock stops while the agents cannot run: a drain stops the whole clock, so the run's minimum and maximum durations leave it out too, not only the progress rule. A trial that runs during the drain still counts what it finds.
- 2026-10-25, owner, from the mockups: item 3's trials show beside each lane, under **At work** (layout B), with the card at the top; item 5 keeps today's bar. A stopped trial's experiment goes back to the top of the queue, as an interrupted trial's does: the lead's proposal, which the owner let stand.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-25 05:23 UTC: Goal written. Items 1 and 2 built, with tests that fail on the old code, and pages updated: Agents, lanes and the planner; Campaign modes; Steer a run; Write a contract; the contract reference; the glossary; Limits and pacing. A side effect to watch on the lab: rounds can end more often than before, when one lane is much faster than another, so a run with the planner on makes more planner calls: at most one for each `trials_per_epoch` trials of its fastest lane, where it was one for each `trials_per_epoch` trials of every lane.

- 2026-10-25 09:20 UTC: Items 1 and 2 are on the lab. In a research run there, one lane started its round-3 trial at 09:18:30; round 4 started at 09:18:55 while that trial ran, and the other lane began its round-4 task at once. The run's sixth question, which had no work for nine hours before, got the first task of round 4, and every question has tasks up next.

- 2026-10-25 09:30 UTC: Item 4 built (branch `fix/136-progress-clock`). The clock's blocked time moved out of `research.ts` into `research/blocked.ts`, with each drain added; the drain's state and its end at start are in `service/drain.ts`. Pages: Campaign modes (the progress rule), Write a contract, Budget and stop time, the contract reference, Limits and pacing, Signed commands (`drain_rig`), Rig lifecycle, Troubleshoot, the roadmap, CHANGELOG. To check on the lab after the roll: a research run's `waited_ms` grows during a drain, and its time without an improvement does not.

- 2026-10-25 09:43 UTC: Found on the lab, from item 1 (a teammate's check): in a research run, round 4 gave its task for one question to a lane still on its round-3 trial, and that held task counted as the question's work in the round, so the free lane's next two trials went to other questions, and the question had no work in the round while the busy lane ran. A held task now counts toward its question's share only while its lane is free; it stays that lane's own. The task's place at the end of the queue does not matter: within the round, the lane's first trial takes it directly, and after, a research task is taken by question first, then queue order. Test: `scheduler-research.test.ts` (fails on the old counting).

- 2026-10-25 09:50 UTC: Mockups for items 3 and 5, for the owner to choose from: the rig's page and the run's page as they are, from sample data, with the new parts. Item 3 in two layouts (one card at the top of the rig's page, or beside each lane under **At work**), each trial with how long it has run, what it last did, a repeated refused request, and **Stop this trial…**, with both answers to the open question. Item 5 in two layouts (its state in one line, or the two conditions with a mark each) at four moments (not in force yet, in force, the clock stopped by a drain, met), beside today's bar. Each lists what the harness would have to report for it.

- 2026-10-25 16:16 UTC: Item 3 built, as the owner chose (layout B), and item 5 closed with today's bar. The harness lists each open trial in its report while it drains, with its task, counters, verified claims and its newest re-ask of a refused page, and records who asked for the drain. `stop_trial` records `trial_stop_asked`; the scheduler's check every second stops that trial (`trial_stopped`), and its experiment goes back to the top of the queue. The rig's page shows the card and the rows, from the report and the trial's lines in Live. Pages: A rig's page, Signed commands, MCP tools, Rig lifecycle, Troubleshoot, Agents, lanes and the planner, the roadmap, CHANGELOG. Also filed: issue #24, the held task that kept a free lane off its question (fixed in 74114f2). To check on the lab after the roll: a drain's card names the trials it waits for, and a stop ends one.
- 2026-10-25 20:28 UTC: A lab rig began a drain at 20:18 on a harness from before item 3: its card can't list the trials yet, so the check above waits for the next drain on a harness that has it. The drain waits for 2 of the judge's jobs (it builds changes in parallel), and the card said `the judge's job`. It now says `the judge's 2 jobs`, with a test and the page.

